    "turn_text": "Well, let's go back to when the Browns left the last time. And the city didn't have any recourse. We lost the team. And we got back here later. The memory of that was fresh in the minds of myself and others. When I got to the Ohio Senate, I had a chance to do something about it to make sure that the people of the Cleveland area never again suffered the ignominy of having a team just pick up and go. So what I did was this Bob. I wrote a law that says, if the team, the owner takes taxpayers money to support the team in any way, shape or form. And in this case, the money has gone. to build a place for the team to play uh... and other concessions that the city has given i said that it could take taxpayers money it comes with a string attached in the string is this with the decide to move later on guess what you have to give the city and or local investors the the chance to buy the team i mean it's that simple and this this law by the way which i call the taxpayer protection act the The media renamed it the Modell law. This law was essentially upheld by a Franklin County Common Police Court, which dismissed a motion by the then owner of the Columbus Crow to knock out the Modell law and not have it apply. The court dismissed that effort to scuttle the Modell law and then the team, the doctor team in Columbus was picked up by another owner, and that owner was Jimmy Haslam. So they understand perfectly what this law means. They took advantage of it in Columbus. And maybe they don't like it now because they have other plans. But this law was essentially upheld in Franklin County Common Police Court. And Bob, I'll tell you, the laws, the laws, the law, and they cannot just pick up and move. They owe something to the City of Cleveland. It did detect there's investors. Now, you know, look, I've listened to your program and I know how you feel about government involvement in business. You know what, I essentially believe the same thing, except for one thing. If you're getting money, you know, if the Browns weren't getting money from the city, that's one thing. But they were getting money, hundreds of millions of dollars, changes the game. And so I don't think they're going to be able to just pick up and move. This is certainly putting the city in a strong negotiating position and uh... i wrote a lot of protect the taxpayers and that's exactly what uh... uh... it's going to happen uh... i hope uh... with the court decision",
